ng the negative constraint excluding superordinate category references used for enumeration or classification, and distinguishing this from phenotype-feature mentions.\n\nChecking the rule's consistency against the entity definitions—SpecificDisease for individuated conditions, DiseaseClass for categories—and confirming it doesn't interfere with Modifier or CompositeMention labeling since it only governs the head mention's classification. I'm deciding to present the output as a clean Markdown list without extra preamble.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Referential-Scope Rule for Disease Nouns:** IF a disease-denoting noun phrase is anchored to one individuated condition, either by a proper name, eponym, acronym, gene, or pathogen identity, or by a patient-level clinical role (the argument of a predicate expressing a patient's presentation, diagnosis, or finding, such as \"presenting with,\" \"found to have,\" or \"diagnosed with\"), even when its head noun is a lexically generic umbrella term and/or carries a subtype or site modifier, THEN label the mention SpecificDisease. Base the decision on what the phrase refers to in context, not on the category-like meaning of its head noun.\n  - **Negative Constraint:** Do NOT apply this rule, and label the mention DiseaseClass, when the phrase is a superordinate category reference that enumerates or classifies kinds of disease (e.g., introduced by \"such as,\" \"including,\" \"types of,\" or a class-membership frame), or when it is a phenotype or feature term listed in a generic, definitional description of a syndrome or entity rather than a condition a specific individual has or is diagnosed with. The presence of a modifier alone does not change the label in either direction.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0,